Instead of storing unmatched braces, the prefix balance can show how many closing braces are out of place. Add one for `{` and subtract one for `}`. The smallest balance gives the number of unmatched closing braces.

If `badClose` is that number, then the unmatched opening count is `finalBalance + badClose`. Each group is fixed by reversing half of its braces, rounded up.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int countRev(string s) {
        if (s.size() % 2 == 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        int balance = 0;
        int smallestBalance = 0;

        for (char bracket : s) {
            if (bracket == '{') {
                balance++;
            } else {
                balance--;
            }
            smallestBalance = min(smallestBalance, balance);
        }

        int unmatchedClose = -smallestBalance;
        int unmatchedOpen = balance + unmatchedClose;

        return (unmatchedClose + 1) / 2 + (unmatchedOpen + 1) / 2;
    }
};
```

For an already balanced input such as `{{}}`, the smallest balance is zero and the final balance is zero, so the result is zero. The method also returns `-1` before scanning any odd-length input.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`, since only a few counters are used.

A closing brace at balance zero cannot be matched by anything before it. Reversing that brace into `{` fixes the current prefix and raises the balance to one. The rest of the scan continues normally.

After the scan, any remaining opening braces are handled in pairs. One reversal fixes two of them, with one extra reversal when their count is odd.

Dry run for `}}{{`:

- First `}`: reverse it, balance becomes `1`.
- Next `}`: balance becomes `0`.
- Two `{` characters leave balance `2`.
- One more reversal is needed, so the answer is `2`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countRev(String s) {
        if (s.length() % 2 == 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        int balance = 0;
        int reversals = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char bracket = s.charAt(i);

            if (bracket == '{') {
                balance++;
            } else if (balance > 0) {
                balance--;
            } else {
                reversals++;
                balance = 1;
            }
        }

        reversals += (balance + 1) / 2;
        return reversals;
    }
}
```

An odd-length input is an important edge case because changing braces does not change the string length. It can never contain equal numbers of opening and closing braces.

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(1)`.

A stack makes the unmatched brackets easy to see. Matching pairs such as `{}` are removed while scanning. At the end, the stack contains some `}` brackets followed by some `{` brackets. Reversing half of each group, rounded up, is enough.

For example, `}}{{` leaves two closing and two opening brackets, so only two reversals are needed.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int countRev(string s) {
        if (s.size() % 2 == 1) {
            return -1;
        }

        stack<char> unmatched;

        for (char bracket : s) {
            if (bracket == '{') {
                unmatched.push(bracket);
            } else if (!unmatched.empty() && unmatched.top() == '{') {
                unmatched.pop();
            } else {
                unmatched.push(bracket);
            }
        }

        int opening = 0;
        int closing = 0;

        while (!unmatched.empty()) {
            if (unmatched.top() == '{') {
                opening++;
            } else {
                closing++;
            }
            unmatched.pop();
        }

        return (opening + 1) / 2 + (closing + 1) / 2;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack.

The scan keeps two counts. An opening brace is stored as an available match. A closing brace uses one available opening brace when possible. Otherwise, it becomes an unmatched closing brace.

The empty string needs zero reversals. An odd length can never be balanced, so that case returns `-1`.

```python
class Solution:
    def countRev(self, s: str) -> int:
        if len(s) % 2 == 1:
            return -1

        unmatched_open = 0
        unmatched_close = 0

        for bracket in s:
            if bracket == '{':
                unmatched_open += 1
            elif unmatched_open > 0:
                unmatched_open -= 1
            else:
                unmatched_close += 1

        return (unmatched_open + 1) // 2 + (unmatched_close + 1) // 2
```

For `}}{{`, the first two characters create two unmatched closing braces, and the last two create two unmatched opening braces. Each side needs one reversal.

Time is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(1)`.

## Solution
```java
import java.util.LinkedList;
class Solution {
    public int countRev(String s) {
        if(s.length() % 2 != 0) return -1;

        int opening=0;
        int closing=0;
        LinkedList<Character> stack = new LinkedList<>();
        for(char c : s.toCharArray()){
            if(c == '('){
                stack.push(c);
                opening++;
            }
            if(c == ')'){
                if(!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek() == '('){
                    stack.pop();
                    opening--;
                }
                else{
                    stack.push(c);
                    closing++;
                }
            }
        }
        return (opening/2) + (opening%2) + (closing/2) + (closing%2);
    }
}

```

